---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AiSummary from '../../components/AiSummary.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { certifications } from '../../data/certifications';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { outcomesSurvey } from '../../data/research';
import bls from '../../data/bls.json';

const rdn = bls.occupations['dietitians-nutritionists'];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Which nutrition certification is worth getting?',
    answer:
      'The RDN credential, if you want clinical practice, hospital employment, or insurance reimbursement. Everything else on this page is either a specialisation layered on top of it, a state licence that runs alongside it, or a continuing education product that competes with it and loses. That is a blunt answer and it is the correct one.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you need a certification to call yourself a nutritionist?',
    answer:
      'It depends entirely on your state. Some states license the title, some certify it, some regulate only its use in specific contexts, and some do not regulate it at all. This variation is why the word nutritionist means a protected professional in one state and nothing in particular in another. Check your own state board before you print a business card.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How much do nutrition certifications cost?',
    answer:
      'The examination fees are the small part. The RDN pathway costs a graduate degree plus supervised practice, which is where the real money goes. Specialty credentials add examination and application fees on top of an active RDN. Low-cost coaching certificates are inexpensive precisely because they carry no accreditation, no supervised practice, and no scope of practice. Price is information here.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is a nutrition coaching certificate worth anything?',
    answer:
      'It is worth something in a narrow set of contexts: fitness settings, wellness programming, and adjacent work where you are not providing medical nutrition therapy. It is worth nothing in a hospital, and any marketing that implies otherwise is misleading you about scope of practice. Buy it knowing which of those two situations you are in.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between certification and licensure?',
    answer:
      'A certification is granted by a professional body and demonstrates competence. A licence is granted by a state and controls whether you may legally practise. The RDN credential is a certification. State licensure is separate, varies by state, and is the thing that actually governs your practice authority. You frequently need both, and people conflate them constantly.'
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Nutrition Certifications and Credentials"
  description={`${certifications.length} nutrition and dietetics credentials compared: eligibility, examination detail, costs, study resources, and honest assessment of what each one actually permits you to do.`}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Certifications</p>
      <h1>Nutrition credentials, and which ones actually carry authority</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {certifications.length} credentials compared on eligibility, examination content, cost, and career effect. Some
        of these change what you are legally permitted to do. Others are continuing education marketed as though they
        do. We are explicit about which is which.
      </p>
      <div class="metrics">
        <div><strong>{certifications.length}</strong><span>credentials covered</span></div>
        <div><strong>{outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty}%</strong><span>of employers report difficulty finding qualified RDNs</span></div>
        <div><strong>${rdn.median.toLocaleString()}</strong><span>median wage, dietitians and nutritionists</span></div>
        <div><strong>2024</strong><span>year the graduate degree requirement took effect</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <AiSummary>
        NutritionSchools.org compares {certifications.length} nutrition and dietetics credentials: the Registered
        Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) from the Commission on Dietetic Registration, the Certified Nutrition Specialist
        (CNS), the Board Certified Specialist in Sports Dietetics (CSSD), the Certified Diabetes Care and Education
        Specialist (CDCES), state licensure as a Licensed Nutritionist, the Certified Nutrition Coach (CNC), and health
        and wellness coaching certification. The RDN is the only one that reliably opens clinical practice, hospital
        employment, and insurance reimbursement nationally, and since January 2024 it requires a graduate degree plus
        ACEND accredited coursework and supervised practice before examination eligibility. Specialty credentials such
        as the CSSD and CDCES sit on top of an active RDN. Coaching certificates carry no scope of practice for medical
        nutrition therapy. Bureau of Labor Statistics data shows dietitians and nutritionists earning a May 2024 median
        of ${rdn.median.toLocaleString()}, and our own {outcomesSurvey.shortName} found
        {' '}{outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty} percent of employers struggling to find qualified RDN
        candidates. Fees and eligibility rules change: confirm with each issuing body. Last reviewed
        {' '}{site.reviewedLabel}.
      </AiSummary>

      <div class="hot-take">
        <strong>The distinction that matters</strong>
        <p>
          There are two kinds of credential in this field. One kind changes what you are legally permitted to do. The
          other kind changes what you are permitted to put on a website. Both are sold in the same tone of voice, at
          wildly different prices, and confusing them costs people years.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Comparison</p>
          <h2>Every credential, side by side</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Issuers set their own rules and change them. Every line here should be confirmed at source.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Credential</th><th>Issuer</th><th>Who it is for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {certifications.map(cert => (
              <tr>
                <td><a href={`/certifications/${cert.slug}/`}><strong>{cert.short}</strong></a></td>
                <td>{cert.issuer}</td>
                <td>{cert.for}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">All credentials</p>
          <h2>Read the one you are considering, in full</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Each page covers eligibility, examination content, real costs, study resources, and career effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        {certifications.map(cert => (
          <a class="card" href={`/certifications/${cert.slug}/`}>
            <span class="label">{cert.short}</span>
            <h3>{cert.title}</h3>
            <p>{cert.takeaway}</p>
            <span class="text-link">Read the credential guide →</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="content wrap">
    <h2>How to think about nutrition credentials</h2>
    <p>
      Sort them into three buckets before you compare anything else, because the buckets determine everything about how
      much a credential is worth to you.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Practice authority.</strong> The RDN credential plus state licensure. This combination is what permits medical nutrition therapy in a clinical setting and what hospital employers screen for. There is no substitute and there is no shortcut.</li>
      <li><strong>Specialisation on top of authority.</strong> The CSSD and CDCES require an active RDN, documented practice experience, and a specialty examination. They increase your value inside a scope you already hold rather than granting a new one.</li>
      <li><strong>Adjacent and unregulated.</strong> Coaching certifications and general nutrition certificates. Useful in fitness, wellness, and corporate settings. Not a route into clinical work, regardless of how the marketing reads.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The CNS sits awkwardly across the first two buckets. It is a legitimate credential with a graduate degree
      requirement, a supervised practice requirement, and an examination, and in some states it supports licensure. It
      is also less widely recognised by employers than the RDN, which matters more than whether it is defensible in
      principle.
    </p>

    <h2>The 2024 change you need to know about</h2>
    <p>
      Since January 2024 the Commission on Dietetic Registration has required a minimum of a graduate degree for
      eligibility to sit the RDN examination. This is the single most consequential change in nutrition credentialing in
      a generation, and it changed the economics of the whole field.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical effect: the pathway is now longer and more expensive, which means the credential is scarcer, which
      means it is worth more to the people who hold it. Our own {outcomesSurvey.shortName} found
      {' '}{outcomesSurvey.headline.employerHiringDifficulty} percent of employers reporting difficulty finding qualified
      RDN candidates. That is a shortage, and a shortage is leverage for anyone who completes the pathway.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rules that change once can change again. Confirm the current requirement directly with the Commission before you
      build a five-year plan on it.
    </p>

    <h2>Certification against licensure</h2>
    <p>
      These are two different things and conflating them is the most common error we see. A certification is granted by
      a professional body and demonstrates competence. A licence is granted by a state and controls whether you may
      legally practise. The RDN credential is a certification. State licensure is separate.
    </p>
    <p>
      State rules vary more than most students expect. Some states license dietitians, some certify them, some regulate
      the title nutritionist and some do not regulate it at all. The only reliable source is your own state board, and
      checking it takes twenty minutes. See the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/certifications/ln/">state licensure guide</a> for how to approach it.
    </p>

    <h2>What credentials are worth in money</h2>
    <p>
      Our {outcomesSurvey.name} recorded median first-position salaries by highest credential held: $52,000 for
      certificate holders, $58,000 at associate level, $65,000 at bachelor level, $75,000 at master level, and $85,000
      at doctoral level. Those are our figures from a self-selected sample rather than a federal statistic.
    </p>
    <p>
      Federal data adds the occupational picture: the Bureau of Labor Statistics reported a May 2024 median annual wage
      of ${rdn.median.toLocaleString()} for dietitians and nutritionists, with the highest 10 percent above
      {' '}${rdn.p90.toLocaleString()}. The credential is what moves you up that distribution, more than the institution
      on your diploma does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next: the <a class="text-link" href="/careers/registered-dietitian-nutritionist/">RDN career guide</a>,
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/best-dietetics-programs/">best dietetics programs</a>, the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/resources/how-to-become-a-registered-dietitian-step-by-step/">RDN pathway</a>,
      and the <a class="text-link" href="/resources/nutrition-career-outcomes/">Career Outcomes Survey 2026</a>.
    </p>

    <Faq items={faq} />

    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Confirm at source:</strong> examination fees, eligibility requirements, continuing education
      requirements, and licensure rules are set by issuing bodies and state boards, and they change. Every credential
      page on this site links to the issuer. Treat that link as the authority and this site as a starting point.
      Nothing here is medical, dietary, or legal advice.
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
